/* book: lead: A reverse-biased p–n junction turns absorbed light into current: each absorbed photon makes an electron–hole pair, and the junction field sweeps the pair out before it recombines. A plain PN diode collects light only in its thin depletion layer, so it is inefficient and slow. Putting a wide intrinsic (i) layer between the p and n sides gives the PIN photodiode, the standard detector of fiber links */
/* book Figure 4.2.1: The worked example's InGaAs PIN: light enters through the coating and the thin p⁺ layer, and its power falls as it is absorbed across the 4 µm i layer, so only 1.8 % is left at the far side. The field below is strong across the whole i layer, so each pair is swept out: electrons to n⁺, holes to p⁺. */
