/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A PN photodiode is a p–n junction held in reverse bias.
   - A pair created in the neutral p or n region feels almost no field.
   - η is the quantum efficiency; r is the fraction of light reflected at the surface; α is the absorption coefficient (in m⁻¹ or cm⁻¹); w is the width of the depleted layer.
   - A PIN photodiode places an undoped (intrinsic) i layer between a heavily doped p⁺ layer and an n⁺ layer.
   - How wide the i layer must be depends on α.
   - A wide i layer lowers the junction capacitance, which helps speed, but carriers take longer to cross it, which hurts speed.
   - Carriers cross the i layer at the saturation velocity vsat, about 1 × 10⁵ m/s in silicon and about 6.5 × 10⁴ m/s for the slower carriers (holes) in InGaAs.
   - The junction is also a parallel-plate capacitor, C = εrε0A/w, with ε0 = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F/m and A the diode area.
   - f3dB ≈ 1/√(1/ftr² + 1/fRC²)
   - The two limits act together, so the diode's overall bandwidth is lower than either of them.
*/
