/* book exam traps: Thinking a thicker i layer is always better. It raises η and lowers C, but it lengthens the transit time and lowers ftr. | Dropping (1 − r). With r = 0.03, 3 % of the light is lost before any absorption. | Mixing units in αw: α in cm⁻¹ needs w in cm, α in m⁻¹ needs w in m. Mixing them gives an exponent 100 times wrong. | Reporting the smaller limit as the bandwidth. The two limits combine, so the real bandwidth (5.9 GHz in the example) is below both. | Forward biasing a photodiode. It must be reverse biased (or at zero bias); forward bias injects its own current and swamps the photocurrent. */
